WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.940
Common privilege-separation architectures use helper processes, broker services, task-specific workers, local agents, installers, or update helpers.

2
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:15.320
These components hold authority on behalf of the application, but they should not become general-purpose tools.

3
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:21.500
A helper should expose a narrow set of commands with clearly documented inputs and outcomes.

4
00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.580
A broker mediates sensitive operations.

5
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:37.400
It receives a request, validates the message, identifies the caller where the platform supports it, applies authorization policy, performs the narrow operation, and records audit-friendly results.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:43.260
The broker should make the decision where the authority exists rather than relying only on earlier checks in the client.

7
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:47.540
Lifecycle behavior matters because helpers are long-lived security components.

8
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:54.200
Startup, shutdown, update, recovery, and version mismatch behavior should be designed deliberately.

9
00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:01.740
A helper should not keep elevated state longer than necessary, and it should fail in a way that leaves protected resources consistent.

10
00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.520
Partial operations need cleanup or a safe recovery plan.

11
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.440
Logging should support operations without exposing secrets or sensitive handles.

12
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:18.940
A useful log says what command was requested, whether it was allowed, which safe resource category was involved, and what result occurred.

13
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.160
It does not need to print private data or internal implementation details to be useful.

14
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:25.880
Helpers need ownership.

15
00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:34.620
Teams should know who reviews command additions, who approves policy changes, who tests deployment behavior, and who responds to failures.

16
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:40.000
Without ownership, privileged components can grow quietly as new feature work accumulates.
